  "id": "DrugBank_DB13679 - MONDO_5492",
  "verbalization": "Dexchlorpheniramine is linked to Urticaria (disease) through a series of indications that suggest a potential role in the development of allergic reactions. The drug is associated with Angioedema, which in turn is linked to Bamipine and Tripelennamine, both of which are indicated to cause Urticaria. Similarly, Dexchlorpheniramine is also linked to Familial Dermatographia, a condition that is indicated to be associated with Tripelennamine and Phenyltoloxamine, both of which are linked to Urticaria. These pathways imply a possible mechanism by which Dexchlorpheniramine may contribute to the development of urticaria through the activation of allergic responses, possibly via the modulation of histamine release or inflammatory pathways. The evidence suggests a chain of indications that could be interpreted as a potential mechanism for the drug's involvement in urticaria, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
